sh.\",\"For every pulse, a flash occurs later and no pulse occurs strictly between the pulse and the flash.\",\"For every pulse, the flash occurs before any subsequent pulse.\"]},{\"review_id\":\"R085\",\"comments\":\"\",\"interpretations\":[\"For every pulse, a flash occurs later and the pause state does not hold between the pulse and the flash.\",\"For every pulse, a flash occurs at the next step, so there is no pause step in between.\",\"For every pulse, a flash occurs later with no intervening pause state.\"]},{\"review_id\":\"R086\",\"comments\":\"\",\"interpretations\":[\"For every pulse, at the first release after it, the light is on.\",\"For every pulse, the next release after it occurs and the light is on at that release.\",\"For every pulse, when the next release occurs, the light is on.\"]},{\"review_id\":\"R087\",\"comments\":\"\",\"interpretations\":[\"At any step where a pulse occurs and enable holds, a flash occurs at some later step.\",\"At any step where a pulse and enable hold, a flash occurs at the next step or later.\",\"For every enabled pulse, a later flash occurs.\"]},{\"review_id\":\"R088\",\"comments\":\"\",\"interpretations\":[\"For every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs later than the pulse and before the first stop.\",\"For every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs later and also before that stop.\",\"Before any stop, every pulse has a later flash before that stop.\"]}]}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need answer JSON with entries for all 88 review_ids, each comments and interpretations array min 1. Need produce model-generated annotations. Need infer task: For each requirement, provide plausible interpretations in own words, including timing/scope. We need not actually fill worksheet times. Need output JSON only. Need cover every review_id. Need comments optional but schema requires comments string. We can put \"\" or brief. Need interpretations array strings. Need likely one or more plausible interpretations per requirement. We need be careful: This is about temporal logic requirement ambiguity. Need provide interpretations. We need generate for each. We can include multiple interpretations where ambiguous. Need not be perfect but plausible. Need all 88. This is long. Need ensure valid JSON. We can use concise strings. Need no markdown? final should be JSON object. Need include entries array with 88 objects. Need comments maybe \"Model-generated annotation.\" or empty. Interpretations should be distinct plausible meanings. Need include timing/scope. We can use formal-ish natural language. Need not include proposed readings (Stage 2 absent). We need produce open interpretations. For each requirement, list plausible interpretations. Many are straightforward. Need avoid leaving blank. We can include \"No plausible interpretation\" only if appropriate, but likely all have plausible. Need maybe include one or two interpretations. Need ensure each array minItems 1. Need comments string. We can set comments \"\" for all. Need JSON valid. Need not include times. Need cover every review_id exactly.\n\nNeed think through each requirement and possible interpretations. We can produce concise but distinct. Need maybe use \"Interpretation 1: ...\" but array items can be plain. Need include timing/scope. We can use \"At some future step, both a pulse and a flash occur (order unspecified).\" etc.\n\nNeed be mindful of temporal logic semantics: \"eventually\", \"always\", \"until\", \"next\", \"infinitely often\", \"from some point\", \"before\", \"after\", \"immediately\", \"consecutive\", \"at same step\", \"strictly later\", \"optional release\", etc. Need list plausible readings. For many, one interpretation enough. But requirement says \"all interpretations you consider plausible\"; we can list 1-3. Need not overdo. But for ambiguous ones, list multiple.\n\nNeed produce 88 entries. Let's draft systematically.\n\nWe need decide comments: maybe \"Model-generated annotation; no additional comments.\" But comments optional. Use \"\" to save. Schema requires comments string. Fine.\n\nNeed ensure no trailing commas. Need valid JSON. Long but manageable.\n\nLet's enumerate R001-R088.\n\nI'll craft interpretations.\n\nR001 \"A pulse and a flash must eventually occur.\" Propositions a pulse, b flash. Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, a pulse occurs and at some (possibly different) future step a flash occurs; order unspecified.\n2. At some single future step, both a pulse and a flash occur simultaneously.\nMaybe \"eventually both occur, not necessarily same step\" and \"eventually both occur at same step\". Good.\n\nR002 \"Pulses and flashes must keep occurring.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often a pulse occurs and infinitely often a flash occurs (not necessarily paired).\n2. From every point onward, at least one pulse and at least one flash occur in the future; i.e., both keep recurring.\n3. A pulse and a flash occur together infinitely often. Maybe.\n\nR003 \"Every request must be followed by a pulse and a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For each request, at some later step a pulse occurs and at some later step a flash occurs; order unspecified.\n2. For each request, a pulse and a flash both occur at some later step, possibly same step.\n3. For each request, a pulse occurs later and then a flash occurs later (pulse before flash). Need include.\n\nR004 \"A pulse and a flash must occur before any stop.\" Interpretations:\n1. If a stop ever occurs, then before the first stop there is a pulse and a flash (order unspecified).\n2. Before every stop, a pulse and a flash occur (for each stop).\n3. A pulse and a flash occur before the first stop, and a stop must occur? Requirement doesn't say stop must occur. \"before any stop\" could be conditional. Include \"if any stop occurs\". Good.\n\nR005 \"After each request, a pulse and a flash are required before the next stop.\" Interpretations:\n1. For each request, if a stop occurs after it, then before that next stop there is a pulse and a flash (order unspecified).\n2. For each request, a pulse and a flash occur after it and before any subsequent stop; if no stop, they still must occur eventually? Ambiguous. Need list.\n3. For each request, a pulse then a flash occur before the next stop. Good.\n\nR006 \"The signal and the indicator must eventually be active for two consecutive steps.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future pair of consecutive steps, both signal and indicator are active at both steps.\n2. At some future step, signal and indicator are active, and at the next step they are active again (same).\n3. Eventually there is a two-step interval where each of signal and indicator is active throughout. Good.\n\nR007 \"The signal and the indicator must eventually become active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, both signal and indicator are active simultaneously.\n2. At some future step signal becomes active and at some (possibly different) future step indicator becomes active; not necessarily simultaneous.\n3. Each eventually becomes active at least once. Good.\n\nR008 \"Eventually the signal and the indicator must be active until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future point, both signal and indicator become active and remain active until a release occurs; release may or may not be required? \"until\" often implies release eventually? Ambiguous. \n2. There is a future interval starting when both are active and ending at a release, with both active throughout; release must occur.\n3. Eventually, from some point, both are active unless/until a release occurs; if no release, they stay active forever. Need list.\n\nR009 \"A pulse and a flash must occur with a completion afterward.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step a pulse and a flash occur (order/same step unspecified), and after both a completion occurs.\n2. A pulse occurs, then a flash occurs, then a completion occurs (strict order).\n3. A pulse and flash occur, and a completion occurs at some later step than the later of the two. Good.\n\nR010 \"After a start, a pulse and a flash must occur before a finish.\" Interpretations:\n1. For each start, if a finish occurs after it, then a pulse and a flash occur after the start and before that finish.\n2. For each start, a pulse and a flash occur after it and before the next finish; finish may be required? \n3. After any start, a pulse and flash occur before any subsequent finish (order unspecified). Good.\n\nR011 \"A pulse immediately followed by a flash and a click immediately followed by a mark must eventually occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future point, a pulse occurs and the next step is a flash; separately, at some future point a click occurs and the next step is a mark.\n2. At some future point, both pairs occur in immediate succession, possibly at different times; order between pairs unspecified.\n3. At some future point, pulse-flash pair occurs and click-mark pair occurs, with each pair on consecutive steps. Good.\n\nR012 \"The signal and the indicator must keep being active for two consecutive steps.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often, there is a pair of consecutive steps at which both signal and indicator are active at both steps.\n2. From every point onward, eventually there is a two-consecutive-step interval where both are active.\n3. The signal and indicator remain active for two consecutive steps repeatedly forever. Good.\n\nR013 \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, at some later step a flash occurs.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the next step.\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the same step or later. Need include \"followed\" usually later, but ambiguous. Good.\n\nR014 \"If there is a pulse now, a flash must follow.\" Interpretations:\n1. At any step where a pulse occurs, a flash occurs at some later step.\n2. At any step where a pulse occurs, a flash occurs at the next step.\n3. At any step where a pulse occurs, a flash occurs at that same step or later. Good.\n\nR015 \"There must be at least one pulse followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step a pulse occurs and at a later step a flash occurs.\n2. There exists a pulse and a flash with pulse before flash (not necessarily first/only).\n3. At some future step a pulse and flash occur with flash at same or later step. Good.\n\nR016 \"After every pulse, the indicator must then stay on permanently.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, from the next step onward the indicator is on forever.\n2. For every pulse, from that same step onward the indicator is on forever.\n3. After the first pulse, the indicator stays on forever (scope could be all pulses). Need list.\n\nR017 \"A stop must occur, and before the first stop every pulse must be followed by a flash, even if that flash comes after the stop.\" Interpretations:\n1. A stop occurs; for every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs at some later step, possibly after the first stop.\n2. A stop occurs; for every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs before or after the stop (no restriction), i.e., just eventually after pulse.\n3. A stop occurs; for every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs at some step (could be after stop), and no requirement on pulses after stop. Good.\n\nR018 \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash, with a chime at some strictly later step than that flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, there is a flash later than the pulse and a chime strictly later than that flash.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs later, and after the flash a chime occurs; chime may be after any flash? Need \"that flash\" means the associated flash. \n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs at next step and a chime at a later step. Good.\n\nR019 \"A pulse must never be followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. It is never the case that a pulse occurs and a flash occurs at any later step.\n2. No pulse is immediately followed by a flash (weaker).\n3. No flash occurs after any pulse; flashes may occur before pulses. Good.\n\nR020 \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash before any subsequent stop, with no stop at the flash step.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, if a stop occurs after it, a flash occurs after the pulse and before that stop; the flash step is not a stop step.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs after it and before the next stop; no stop at the flash step.\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs before any later stop, and the flash and stop cannot coincide. Good.\n\nR021 \"Every pair of pulses on consecutive steps must be followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. Whenever pulses occur at two consecutive steps, a flash occurs at some later step after the second pulse.\n2. Whenever pulses occur at two consecutive steps, a flash occurs at the next step after the pair.\n3. Whenever there are consecutive pulses, a flash occurs at some step after the first pulse (possibly during/after pair). Need list.\n\nR022 \"Eventually, the rule that every pulse is followed by a flash must hold from then on.\" Interpretations:\n1. There is a future step from which, for all later pulses, a flash occurs later.\n2. There is a future step from which every pulse is immediately followed by a flash.\n3. Eventually the system enters a state where all pulses (whenever they occur) have a later flash. Good.\n\nR023 \"A pulse followed by a flash must occur over and over without end.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often, a pulse occurs and later a flash occurs.\n2. Infinitely often, a pulse is immediately followed by a flash.\n3. From every point onward, there is a future pulse-flash pair. Good.\n\nR024 \"Once an activation occurs, every pulse from that step onward must be followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. After the first activation, every pulse at or after that step has a later flash.\n2. After any activation, every pulse from that activation step onward has a later flash (for each activation).\n3. After activation, every pulse is immediately followed by a flash. Good.\n\nR025 \"Requests require an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. At every step where a request occurs, the response is active at that same step.\n2. For every request, the response is active at some later step.\n3. For every request, the response is active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR026 \"A request at the starting step requires an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. If a request occurs at the initial step, the response is active at that initial step.\n2. If a request occurs at the initial step, the response is active at some later step.\n3. If a request occurs at the initial step, the response is active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR027 \"There must be a request with an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, a request occurs and the response is active at that same step.\n2. There exists a request and a response active at some (possibly different) step associated with it.\n3. At some future step, a request occurs and the response is active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR028 \"Requests with an active response must keep occurring.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often, a request occurs while the response is active at the same step.\n2. From every point onward, there is a future step with a request and active response.\n3. Requests and active responses recur together infinitely often. Good.\n\nR029 \"Eventually, requests must always have an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. There is a future step from which every request has the response active at the same step.\n2. There is a future step from which every request has the response active at some later step.\n3. Eventually, whenever a request occurs, the response is active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR030 \"Requests require an active response until a release occurs, and a release must occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. Until the first release, every request has the response active at the same step; a release eventually occurs.\n2. Until a release, every request has the response active at that step or later; release required.\n3. For every request before a release, response active at request step; release must occur. Good.\n\nR031 \"Requests require an active response unless and until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. Before any release, every request has the response active at the same step; after release no requirement; release optional.\n2. Until a release occurs, every request has response active at that step or later; if no release, requirement holds forever.\n3. Requests require active response until release; release may never occur. Good.\n\nR032 \"From the first release onward, requests require an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. After the first release, every request has the response active at the same step.\n2. After the first release, every request has the response active at some later step.\n3. From the first release onward, whenever a request occurs, response active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR033 \"Every request requires a response that remains active forever.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, from the request step onward the response is active forever.\n2. For every request, from some later step after the request the response is active forever.\n3. For every request, the response is active at the request step and stays active forever. Good.\n\nR034 \"Every request requires the response to stay active until a release, which must occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, the response stays active from the request step until a release occurs; a release eventually occurs.\n2. For every request, response active at request step and remains until some release; release required after each request? \n3. For every request, response active until the next release, and a release must occur. Good.\n\nR035 \"A request at the next step requires an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. If a request occurs at the next step, the response is active at that next step.\n2. If a request occurs at the next step, the response is active at the step after that.\n3. If a request occurs at the next step, response active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR036 \"Requests made while the response is permitted require an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. At any step where a request occurs and the response is permitted, the response is active at that same step.\n2. At any step where a request occurs and response permitted, response active at some later step.\n3. At any step where request and permission hold, response active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR037 \"A pulse is to be followed by a flash, possibly at the same step.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the same step or a later step.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs at some later step (same-step not allowed).\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the next step or later. Good.\n\nR038 \"A pulse requires the light to be on at that step.\" Interpretations:\n1. At every step where a pulse occurs, the light is on at that same step.\n2. At every step where a pulse occurs, the light is on at that step or later.\n3. A pulse implies light on at the pulse step (same). Good.\n\nR039 \"A pulse is to have a flash at the next step.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the immediately following step.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the next step or later (weaker).\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the same step or next step. Good.\n\nR040 \"A pulse requires the light to stay on from that step onward.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, from the pulse step onward the light is on forever.\n2. For every pulse, from the next step onward the light is on forever.\n3. After the first pulse, light stays on forever. Good.\n\nR041 \"A pulse calls for the light to be on until a release occurs, with a release required.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, the light is on from the pulse step until a release occurs; a release eventually occurs.\n2. For every pulse, light on from pulse step until the next release; release required.\n3. For every pulse, light stays on until some release, and a release must occur. Good.\n\nR042 \"A pulse calls for the light to stay on until a release, or forever if no release comes.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, light is on from the pulse step until a release if one occurs; if no release, light stays on forever.\n2. For every pulse, light on until the next release; release optional.\n3. For every pulse, light remains on unless/until a release occurs. Good.\n\nR043 \"A pulse calls for the light to be on at the first release at or after that pulse, and that release must occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, there is a release at or after the pulse, and the light is on at that release step.\n2. For every pulse, the first release at or after it occurs and light is on at that step.\n3. For every pulse, a release eventually occurs and light is on at the first such release. Good.\n\nR044 \"A pulse calls for a release before any flash from that step onward, and a flash at or after the release.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, from the pulse step onward, a release occurs before any flash; and a flash occurs at or after that release.\n2. For every pulse, no flash occurs until a release occurs; after release a flash occurs.\n3. For every pulse, the next flash is preceded by a release, and a flash occurs at/after release. Good.\n\nR045 \"A pulse while enabled calls for a flash at that step or later.\" Interpretations:\n1. At any step where a pulse occurs and enable holds, a flash occurs at that same step or later.\n2. At any step where pulse and enable hold, a flash occurs at some later step.\n3. At any step where pulse and enable hold, a flash occurs at that step or next step. Good.\n\nR046 \"Before the first stop, a pulse calls for a flash at that step or later.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs at the pulse step or later (possibly after stop).\n2. For every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs at the pulse step or later but before the first stop.\n3. For every pulse before first stop, a flash occurs at that step or later; no stop required. Good.\n\nR047 \"The first and second indicators must not be active now.\" Interpretations:\n1. At the initial/current step, neither the first nor the second indicator is active.\n2. At the current step, it is not the case that both are active (at least one inactive).\n3. At the current step, both are inactive. Good.\n\nR048 \"The first and second indicators must never be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At no step are both first and second indicators active simultaneously.\n2. At no step is either indicator active.\n3. Neither indicator is ever active (both always inactive). Good.\n\nR049 \"At some point, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, neither indicator is active.\n2. At some future step, it is not the case that both are active (at least one inactive).\n3. At some future step, both are inactive simultaneously. Good.\n\nR050 \"Infinitely often, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often, neither indicator is active.\n2. Infinitely often, it is not the case that both are active.\n3. Infinitely often, both are inactive at the same step. Good.\n\nR051 \"From some point onward, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. There is a future step from which neither indicator is ever active.\n2. There is a future step from which it is never the case that both are active.\n3. Eventually both indicators remain inactive forever. Good.\n\nR052 \"The first and second indicators must not be active until a release occurs, and a release must occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. Until the first release, neither indicator is active; a release eventually occurs.\n2. Until a release, it is not the case that both are active; release required.\n3. Before release both indicators inactive; release must occur. Good.\n\nR053 \"The first and second indicators must not be active until a release occurs; release is optional.\" Interpretations:\n1. Until a release occurs, neither indicator is active; if no release, they stay inactive forever.\n2. Until a release, it is not the case that both are active; release optional.\n3. Before any release both inactive; release may never occur. Good.\n\nR054 \"Whenever the gate is open, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At every step where the gate is open, neither indicator is active.\n2. At every step where gate open, it is not the case that both are active.\n3. While gate open, both indicators inactive. Good.\n\nR055 \"Whenever a pulse occurs, the first and second indicators must not be active at the immediately following step.\" Interpretations:\n1. At the step immediately after any pulse, neither indicator is active.\n2. At the step immediately after any pulse, it is not the case that both are active.\n3. At the next step after a pulse, both indicators inactive. Good.\n\nR056 \"Whenever a pulse occurs, there must be a step at that time or later when the first and second indicators are not active.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, at some step at or after the pulse, neither indicator is active.\n2. For every pulse, at some step at or after the pulse, it is not the case that both are active.\n3. For every pulse, eventually after it both indicators are inactive. Good.\n\nR057 \"Whenever a pulse occurs, the first and second indicators must not be active until a release occurs at that step or later, and such a release is required.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, from the pulse step until a release at or after the pulse, neither indicator is active; a release eventually occurs.\n2. For every pulse, indicators not both active until a release at/after pulse; release required.\n3. For every pulse, both indicators inactive until the next release at/after pulse, and that release must occur. Good.\n\nR058 \"A check must occur at a step when the first and second indicators are not active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, a check occurs and neither indicator is active at that same step.\n2. At some future step, a check occurs and it is not the case that both indicators are active.\n3. There is a check step where both indicators are inactive. Good.\n\nR059 \"Requests are answered by a pulse or a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, a pulse or a flash occurs at some later step.\n2. For every request, a pulse or a flash occurs at the same step or later.\n3. For every request, either a pulse or a flash (or both) occurs at the next step. Good.\n\nR060 \"After a request, the next step must contain a pulse or a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, at the immediately following step a pulse or a flash occurs.\n2. For every request, at the next step either a pulse or a flash (or both) occurs.\n3. For every request, the next step has at least one of pulse/flash. Good.\n\nR061 \"A request must be answered at once by a pulse or a flash, but not both.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, at the next step exactly one of pulse or flash occurs.\n2. For every request, at the same step exactly one of pulse or flash occurs.\n3. For every request, immediately after it either a pulse or a flash occurs, not both. Good.\n\nR062 \"Every request starts a pulse-then-flash or flash-then-pulse response, with the two events on consecutive steps.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, either a pulse occurs then a flash at the next step, or a flash occurs then a pulse at the next step.\n2. For every request, the response begins at the next step with one event and the other at the following step.\n3. For every request, there is a consecutive pair pulse-flash or flash-pulse after it. Good.\n\nR063 \"A request requires the first or second signal to stay active until a release, which must eventually occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, at least one of first/second signal is active from the request step until a release; release eventually occurs.\n2. For every request, either first or second signal remains active until the next release; release required.\n3. For every request, one of the signals stays active until some release, and a release must occur. Good.\n\nR064 \"A request requires the first or second signal to remain active unless released; release is optional.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, at least one signal stays active until a release if one occurs; if no release, it stays active forever.\n2. For every request, one of the signals remains active unless/until release; release optional.\n3. For every request, first or second signal active until release; release may never occur. Good.\n\nR065 \"Until a stop occurs, requests must receive an immediate pulse or flash; a stop must eventually occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. Before the first stop, every request is followed at the next step by a pulse or flash; a stop eventually occurs.\n2. Until a stop, every request gets an immediate pulse/flash; stop required.\n3. Before any stop, requests answered immediately; stop must occur. Good.\n\nR066 \"Unless a stop occurs, requests must receive an immediate pulse or flash; a stop is not required.\" Interpretations:\n1. If no stop ever occurs, every request is followed at the next step by a pulse or flash; stop optional.\n2. Until a stop (if any), requests get immediate pulse/flash; stop not required.\n3. Requests always get immediate pulse/flash unless a stop occurs; stop may never occur. Good.\n\nR067 \"Requests must receive a pulse or flash before the next stop.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, if a stop occurs after it, a pulse or flash occurs after the request and before that next stop.\n2. For every request, a pulse or flash occurs before any subsequent stop; if no stop, no requirement? \n3. For every request, a pulse/flash occurs before the next stop (stop may be required? no). Good.\n\nR068 \"The state must stay active until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. From now until the first release, the state is active; if no release, state stays active forever.\n2. The state remains active until a release occurs; release may or may not be required.\n3. The state is active at every step before a release. Good.\n\nR069 \"Whenever a request occurs, the state must remain active until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, the state is active from the request step until a release occurs; if no release, active forever.\n2. For every request, state active until the next release; release optional.\n3. For every request, state remains active unless/until release. Good.\n\nR070 \"Eventually, the state must stay active until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. There is a future step from which the state stays active until a release occurs; if no release, active forever.\n2. Eventually the state becomes active and remains until a release; release optional.\n3. At some future point, state active until release. Good.\n\nR071 \"From every step onward, the state must stay active until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. At every step, the state is active from that step until a release occurs (if any); if no release, active forever.\n2. From any point, state remains active until release; release optional.\n3. The state is always active unless/until a release. Good.\n\nR072 \"The state must remain active and the indicator lit until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. From now until a release, both state active and indicator lit; release optional.\n2. Until release, state active and indicator lit; release required? Ambiguous.\n3. Both conditions hold at every step before release. Good.\n\nR073 \"The state must remain active until a release or a cancellation occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. State active until either a release or cancellation occurs; if neither, active forever.\n2. State active until the first of release/cancellation; either may end it.\n3. State remains active unless/until release or cancellation. Good.\n\nR074 \"The state must remain active until a release occurs while the indicator is lit.\" Interpretations:\n1. State active until a release, and at that release step the indicator is lit.\n2. State active until a release occurs; during that interval indicator is lit? Ambiguous.\n3. State active until a release that occurs while indicator lit. Good.\n\nR075 \"The state must remain active until the indicator stays lit permanently.\" Interpretations:\n1. State active until a point from which the indicator is lit forever; then no requirement.\n2. State active until the indicator becomes permanently lit; if never, state active forever.\n3. State remains active until indicator is permanently on. Good.\n\nR076 \"Whenever a request occurs, starting at the next step the state must remain active until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, from the step after the request until a release, state active; release optional.\n2. For every request, state active starting next step until release; if no release, active forever.\n3. For every request, next-step onward state active until release. Good.\n\nR077 \"Until a release occurs, every pulse must be followed by a flash at the next step.\" Interpretations:\n1. Before the first release, every pulse has a flash at the immediately following step.\n2. Until a release, every pulse is followed by a flash at the next step; after release no requirement.\n3. Before any release, pulse implies next-step flash. Good.\n\nR078 \"The first state must remain active until a handoff starts a second phase in which the second state remains active and a release must eventually end that phase.\" Interpretations:\n1. First state active until a handoff; after handoff second state active until a release; release eventually occurs.\n2. First state active until handoff; then second state active until release; release required to end phase.\n3. Handoff transitions from first active to second active, and release eventually ends second phase. Good.\n\nR079 \"A handoff must eventually occur with the first state active beforehand; from that handoff the second state must remain active until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. Eventually a handoff occurs while first state active; after it second state active until a release; release optional? \n2. Eventually handoff occurs with first state active before; from handoff second state active until release; release required? Ambiguous.\n3. There is a future handoff preceded by first state active, then second state active until release. Good.\n\nR080 \"A pulse is to be followed later by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, a flash occurs at some strictly later step.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the next step or later.\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs later (not same step). Good.\n\nR081 \"After a pulse, the light is to stay on from the next step onward.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, from the step after the pulse onward the light is on forever.\n2. For every pulse, from the next step onward light stays on (at least until something? no).\n3. After any pulse, light on permanently starting next step. Good.\n\nR082 \"After a pulse, the light is to settle into staying on at some later step.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, there is a later step from which the light stays on forever.\n2. For every pulse, eventually after it the light becomes permanently on.\n3. After a pulse, at some future point light stays on from then on. Good.\n\nR083 \"A pulse is to be followed later by a flash and, later still, a chime.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, a flash occurs later, and a chime occurs strictly later than that flash.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs later and a chime occurs later than the pulse (order between flash/chime unspecified? but \"later still\" means after flash).\n3. For every pulse, pulse < flash < chime. Good.\n\nR084 \"After a pulse, a flash is to occur later with no pulse in between.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, there is a later flash such that no pulse occurs between that pulse and the flash.\n2. For every pulse, the next pulse? Actually no pulse in between means the flash occurs before any subsequent pulse.\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs later and no pulse occurs strictly between. Good.\n\nR085 \"After a pulse, a flash is to occur later without a pause in between.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, a flash occurs later and the pause state does not hold between the pulse and flash.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the next step (no pause step in between).\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs later with no intervening pause state. Good.\n\nR086 \"After a pulse, the next release is to occur with the light on.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, at the first release after it, the light is on.\n2. For every pulse, the next release after it occurs and light is on at that release.\n3. For every pulse, when the next release occurs, light is on. Good.\n\nR087 \"A pulse while enabled is to be followed later by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. At any step where pulse and enable hold, a flash occurs at some later step.\n2. At any step where pulse and enable hold, a flash occurs at the next step or later.\n3. For every enabled pulse, a later flash occurs. Good.\n\nR088 \"Before the first stop, a pulse is to be followed later by a flash, also before that stop.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs later than the pulse and before the first stop.\n2. For every pulse before first stop, a flash occurs later and also before that stop; stop may or may not occur? \"first stop\" implies if stop occurs.\n3. Before any stop, every pulse has a later flash before that stop. Good.\n\nNeed output JSON. Need ensure all 88. Need maybe comments empty. Need no extra text. Need valid JSON. Need check array length. We'll produce. Need maybe include \"comments\": \"\" for each. That's fine. Need ensure strings escape quotes? Avoid quotes inside. Use semicolons. Need no newline issues? JSON can be one line but long. Fine.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Model-generated annotation.\" in comments? Empty okay. Schema requires comments string. Use \"\".\n\nNeed ensure interpretations arrays minItems 1. We'll include 2-3 each. Need not too verbose. Need all review_ids. Let's craft final JSON. Need be careful with commas. We'll write compact but readable. Need no markdown. Let's produce.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer JSON with entries for all 88 review_ids, each comments and interpretations array min 1. Need produce model-generated annotations. Need infer task: For each requirement, provide plausible interpretations in own words, including timing/scope. We need not actually fill worksheet times. Need output JSON only. Need cover every review_id. Need comments optional but schema requires comments string. We can put \"\" or brief. Need interpretations array strings. Need likely one or more plausible interpretations per requirement. We need be careful: This is about temporal logic requirement ambiguity. Need provide interpretations. We need generate for each. We can include multiple interpretations where ambiguous. Need not be perfect but plausible. Need all 88. This is long. Need ensure valid JSON. We can use concise strings. Need no markdown? final should be JSON object. Need include entries array with 88 objects. Need comments maybe \"Model-generated annotation.\" or empty. Interpretations should be distinct plausible meanings. Need include timing/scope. We can use formal-ish natural language. Need not include proposed readings (Stage 2 absent). We need produce open interpretations. For each requirement, list plausible interpretations. Many are straightforward. Need avoid leaving blank. We can include \"No plausible interpretation\" only if appropriate, but likely all have plausible. Need maybe include one or two interpretations. Need ensure each array minItems 1. Need comments string. We can set comments \"\" for all. Need JSON valid. Need not include times. Need cover every review_id exactly.\n\nNeed think through each requirement and possible interpretations. We can produce concise but distinct. Need maybe use \"Interpretation 1: ...\" but array items can be plain. Need include timing/scope. We can use \"At some future step, both a pulse and a flash occur (order unspecified).\" etc.\n\nNeed be mindful of temporal logic semantics: \"eventually\", \"always\", \"until\", \"next\", \"infinitely often\", \"from some point\", \"before\", \"after\", \"immediately\", \"consecutive\", \"at same step\", \"strictly later\", \"optional release\", etc. Need list plausible readings. For many, one interpretation enough. But requirement says \"all interpretations you consider plausible\"; we can list 1-3. Need not overdo. But for ambiguous ones, list multiple.\n\nNeed produce 88 entries. Let's draft systematically.\n\nWe need decide comments: maybe \"Model-generated annotation; no additional comments.\" But comments optional. Use \"\" to save. Schema requires comments string. Fine.\n\nNeed ensure no trailing commas. Need valid JSON. Long but manageable.\n\nLet's enumerate R001-R088.\n\nI'll craft interpretations.\n\nR001 \"A pulse and a flash must eventually occur.\" Propositions a pulse, b flash. Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, a pulse occurs and at some (possibly different) future step a flash occurs; order unspecified.\n2. At some single future step, both a pulse and a flash occur simultaneously.\nMaybe \"eventually both occur, not necessarily same step\" and \"eventually both occur at same step\". Good.\n\nR002 \"Pulses and flashes must keep occurring.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often a pulse occurs and infinitely often a flash occurs (not necessarily paired).\n2. From every point onward, at least one pulse and at least one flash occur in the future; i.e., both keep recurring.\n3. A pulse and a flash occur together infinitely often. Maybe.\n\nR003 \"Every request must be followed by a pulse and a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For each request, at some later step a pulse occurs and at some later step a flash occurs; order unspecified.\n2. For each request, a pulse and a flash both occur at some later step, possibly same step.\n3. For each request, a pulse occurs later and then a flash occurs later (pulse before flash). Need include.\n\nR004 \"A pulse and a flash must occur before any stop.\" Interpretations:\n1. If a stop ever occurs, then before the first stop there is a pulse and a flash (order unspecified).\n2. Before every stop, a pulse and a flash occur (for each stop).\n3. A pulse and a flash occur before the first stop, and a stop must occur? Requirement doesn't say stop must occur. \"before any stop\" could be conditional. Include \"if any stop occurs\". Good.\n\nR005 \"After each request, a pulse and a flash are required before the next stop.\" Interpretations:\n1. For each request, if a stop occurs after it, then before that next stop there is a pulse and a flash (order unspecified).\n2. For each request, a pulse and a flash occur after it and before any subsequent stop; if no stop, they still must occur eventually? Ambiguous. Need list.\n3. For each request, a pulse then a flash occur before the next stop. Good.\n\nR006 \"The signal and the indicator must eventually be active for two consecutive steps.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future pair of consecutive steps, both signal and indicator are active at both steps.\n2. At some future step, signal and indicator are active, and at the next step they are active again (same).\n3. Eventually there is a two-step interval where each of signal and indicator is active throughout. Good.\n\nR007 \"The signal and the indicator must eventually become active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, both signal and indicator are active simultaneously.\n2. At some future step signal becomes active and at some (possibly different) future step indicator becomes active; not necessarily simultaneous.\n3. Each eventually becomes active at least once. Good.\n\nR008 \"Eventually the signal and the indicator must be active until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future point, both signal and indicator become active and remain active until a release occurs; release may or may not be required? \"until\" often implies release eventually? Ambiguous. \n2. There is a future interval starting when both are active and ending at a release, with both active throughout; release must occur.\n3. Eventually, from some point, both are active unless/until a release occurs; if no release, they stay active forever. Need list.\n\nR009 \"A pulse and a flash must occur with a completion afterward.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step a pulse and a flash occur (order/same step unspecified), and after both a completion occurs.\n2. A pulse occurs, then a flash occurs, then a completion occurs (strict order).\n3. A pulse and flash occur, and a completion occurs at some later step than the later of the two. Good.\n\nR010 \"After a start, a pulse and a flash must occur before a finish.\" Interpretations:\n1. For each start, if a finish occurs after it, then a pulse and a flash occur after the start and before that finish.\n2. For each start, a pulse and a flash occur after it and before the next finish; finish may be required? \n3. After any start, a pulse and flash occur before any subsequent finish (order unspecified). Good.\n\nR011 \"A pulse immediately followed by a flash and a click immediately followed by a mark must eventually occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future point, a pulse occurs and the next step is a flash; separately, at some future point a click occurs and the next step is a mark.\n2. At some future point, both pairs occur in immediate succession, possibly at different times; order between pairs unspecified.\n3. At some future point, pulse-flash pair occurs and click-mark pair occurs, with each pair on consecutive steps. Good.\n\nR012 \"The signal and the indicator must keep being active for two consecutive steps.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often, there is a pair of consecutive steps at which both signal and indicator are active at both steps.\n2. From every point onward, eventually there is a two-consecutive-step interval where both are active.\n3. The signal and indicator remain active for two consecutive steps repeatedly forever. Good.\n\nR013 \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, at some later step a flash occurs.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the next step.\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the same step or later. Need include \"followed\" usually later, but ambiguous. Good.\n\nR014 \"If there is a pulse now, a flash must follow.\" Interpretations:\n1. At any step where a pulse occurs, a flash occurs at some later step.\n2. At any step where a pulse occurs, a flash occurs at the next step.\n3. At any step where a pulse occurs, a flash occurs at that same step or later. Good.\n\nR015 \"There must be at least one pulse followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step a pulse occurs and at a later step a flash occurs.\n2. There exists a pulse and a flash with pulse before flash (not necessarily first/only).\n3. At some future step a pulse and flash occur with flash at same or later step. Good.\n\nR016 \"After every pulse, the indicator must then stay on permanently.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, from the next step onward the indicator is on forever.\n2. For every pulse, from that same step onward the indicator is on forever.\n3. After the first pulse, the indicator stays on forever (scope could be all pulses). Need list.\n\nR017 \"A stop must occur, and before the first stop every pulse must be followed by a flash, even if that flash comes after the stop.\" Interpretations:\n1. A stop occurs; for every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs at some later step, possibly after the first stop.\n2. A stop occurs; for every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs before or after the stop (no restriction), i.e., just eventually after pulse.\n3. A stop occurs; for every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs at some step (could be after stop), and no requirement on pulses after stop. Good.\n\nR018 \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash, with a chime at some strictly later step than that flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, there is a flash later than the pulse and a chime strictly later than that flash.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs later, and after the flash a chime occurs; chime may be after any flash? Need \"that flash\" means the associated flash. \n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs at next step and a chime at a later step. Good.\n\nR019 \"A pulse must never be followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. It is never the case that a pulse occurs and a flash occurs at any later step.\n2. No pulse is immediately followed by a flash (weaker).\n3. No flash occurs after any pulse; flashes may occur before pulses. Good.\n\nR020 \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash before any subsequent stop, with no stop at the flash step.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, if a stop occurs after it, a flash occurs after the pulse and before that stop; the flash step is not a stop step.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs after it and before the next stop; no stop at the flash step.\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs before any later stop, and the flash and stop cannot coincide. Good.\n\nR021 \"Every pair of pulses on consecutive steps must be followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. Whenever pulses occur at two consecutive steps, a flash occurs at some later step after the second pulse.\n2. Whenever pulses occur at two consecutive steps, a flash occurs at the next step after the pair.\n3. Whenever there are consecutive pulses, a flash occurs at some step after the first pulse (possibly during/after pair). Need list.\n\nR022 \"Eventually, the rule that every pulse is followed by a flash must hold from then on.\" Interpretations:\n1. There is a future step from which, for all later pulses, a flash occurs later.\n2. There is a future step from which every pulse is immediately followed by a flash.\n3. Eventually the system enters a state where all pulses (whenever they occur) have a later flash. Good.\n\nR023 \"A pulse followed by a flash must occur over and over without end.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often, a pulse occurs and later a flash occurs.\n2. Infinitely often, a pulse is immediately followed by a flash.\n3. From every point onward, there is a future pulse-flash pair. Good.\n\nR024 \"Once an activation occurs, every pulse from that step onward must be followed by a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. After the first activation, every pulse at or after that step has a later flash.\n2. After any activation, every pulse from that activation step onward has a later flash (for each activation).\n3. After activation, every pulse is immediately followed by a flash. Good.\n\nR025 \"Requests require an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. At every step where a request occurs, the response is active at that same step.\n2. For every request, the response is active at some later step.\n3. For every request, the response is active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR026 \"A request at the starting step requires an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. If a request occurs at the initial step, the response is active at that initial step.\n2. If a request occurs at the initial step, the response is active at some later step.\n3. If a request occurs at the initial step, the response is active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR027 \"There must be a request with an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, a request occurs and the response is active at that same step.\n2. There exists a request and a response active at some (possibly different) step associated with it.\n3. At some future step, a request occurs and the response is active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR028 \"Requests with an active response must keep occurring.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often, a request occurs while the response is active at the same step.\n2. From every point onward, there is a future step with a request and active response.\n3. Requests and active responses recur together infinitely often. Good.\n\nR029 \"Eventually, requests must always have an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. There is a future step from which every request has the response active at the same step.\n2. There is a future step from which every request has the response active at some later step.\n3. Eventually, whenever a request occurs, the response is active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR030 \"Requests require an active response until a release occurs, and a release must occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. Until the first release, every request has the response active at the same step; a release eventually occurs.\n2. Until a release, every request has the response active at that step or later; release required.\n3. For every request before a release, response active at request step; release must occur. Good.\n\nR031 \"Requests require an active response unless and until a release occurs.\" Interpretations:\n1. Before any release, every request has the response active at the same step; after release no requirement; release optional.\n2. Until a release occurs, every request has response active at that step or later; if no release, requirement holds forever.\n3. Requests require active response until release; release may never occur. Good.\n\nR032 \"From the first release onward, requests require an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. After the first release, every request has the response active at the same step.\n2. After the first release, every request has the response active at some later step.\n3. From the first release onward, whenever a request occurs, response active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR033 \"Every request requires a response that remains active forever.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, from the request step onward the response is active forever.\n2. For every request, from some later step after the request the response is active forever.\n3. For every request, the response is active at the request step and stays active forever. Good.\n\nR034 \"Every request requires the response to stay active until a release, which must occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, the response stays active from the request step until a release occurs; a release eventually occurs.\n2. For every request, response active at request step and remains until some release; release required after each request? \n3. For every request, response active until the next release, and a release must occur. Good.\n\nR035 \"A request at the next step requires an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. If a request occurs at the next step, the response is active at that next step.\n2. If a request occurs at the next step, the response is active at the step after that.\n3. If a request occurs at the next step, response active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR036 \"Requests made while the response is permitted require an active response.\" Interpretations:\n1. At any step where a request occurs and the response is permitted, the response is active at that same step.\n2. At any step where a request occurs and response permitted, response active at some later step.\n3. At any step where request and permission hold, response active at that step or later. Good.\n\nR037 \"A pulse is to be followed by a flash, possibly at the same step.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the same step or a later step.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs at some later step (same-step not allowed).\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the next step or later. Good.\n\nR038 \"A pulse requires the light to be on at that step.\" Interpretations:\n1. At every step where a pulse occurs, the light is on at that same step.\n2. At every step where a pulse occurs, the light is on at that step or later.\n3. A pulse implies light on at the pulse step (same). Good.\n\nR039 \"A pulse is to have a flash at the next step.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the immediately following step.\n2. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the next step or later (weaker).\n3. For every pulse, a flash occurs at the same step or next step. Good.\n\nR040 \"A pulse requires the light to stay on from that step onward.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, from the pulse step onward the light is on forever.\n2. For every pulse, from the next step onward the light is on forever.\n3. After the first pulse, light stays on forever. Good.\n\nR041 \"A pulse calls for the light to be on until a release occurs, with a release required.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, the light is on from the pulse step until a release occurs; a release eventually occurs.\n2. For every pulse, light on from pulse step until the next release; release required.\n3. For every pulse, light stays on until some release, and a release must occur. Good.\n\nR042 \"A pulse calls for the light to stay on until a release, or forever if no release comes.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, light is on from the pulse step until a release if one occurs; if no release, light stays on forever.\n2. For every pulse, light on until the next release; release optional.\n3. For every pulse, light remains on unless/until a release occurs. Good.\n\nR043 \"A pulse calls for the light to be on at the first release at or after that pulse, and that release must occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, there is a release at or after the pulse, and the light is on at that release step.\n2. For every pulse, the first release at or after it occurs and light is on at that step.\n3. For every pulse, a release eventually occurs and light is on at the first such release. Good.\n\nR044 \"A pulse calls for a release before any flash from that step onward, and a flash at or after the release.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, from the pulse step onward, a release occurs before any flash; and a flash occurs at or after that release.\n2. For every pulse, no flash occurs until a release occurs; after release a flash occurs.\n3. For every pulse, the next flash is preceded by a release, and a flash occurs at/after release. Good.\n\nR045 \"A pulse while enabled calls for a flash at that step or later.\" Interpretations:\n1. At any step where a pulse occurs and enable holds, a flash occurs at that same step or later.\n2. At any step where pulse and enable hold, a flash occurs at some later step.\n3. At any step where pulse and enable hold, a flash occurs at that step or next step. Good.\n\nR046 \"Before the first stop, a pulse calls for a flash at that step or later.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs at the pulse step or later (possibly after stop).\n2. For every pulse before the first stop, a flash occurs at the pulse step or later but before the first stop.\n3. For every pulse before first stop, a flash occurs at that step or later; no stop required. Good.\n\nR047 \"The first and second indicators must not be active now.\" Interpretations:\n1. At the initial/current step, neither the first nor the second indicator is active.\n2. At the current step, it is not the case that both are active (at least one inactive).\n3. At the current step, both are inactive. Good.\n\nR048 \"The first and second indicators must never be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At no step are both first and second indicators active simultaneously.\n2. At no step is either indicator active.\n3. Neither indicator is ever active (both always inactive). Good.\n\nR049 \"At some point, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, neither indicator is active.\n2. At some future step, it is not the case that both are active (at least one inactive).\n3. At some future step, both are inactive simultaneously. Good.\n\nR050 \"Infinitely often, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. Infinitely often, neither indicator is active.\n2. Infinitely often, it is not the case that both are active.\n3. Infinitely often, both are inactive at the same step. Good.\n\nR051 \"From some point onward, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. There is a future step from which neither indicator is ever active.\n2. There is a future step from which it is never the case that both are active.\n3. Eventually both indicators remain inactive forever. Good.\n\nR052 \"The first and second indicators must not be active until a release occurs, and a release must occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. Until the first release, neither indicator is active; a release eventually occurs.\n2. Until a release, it is not the case that both are active; release required.\n3. Before release both indicators inactive; release must occur. Good.\n\nR053 \"The first and second indicators must not be active until a release occurs; release is optional.\" Interpretations:\n1. Until a release occurs, neither indicator is active; if no release, they stay inactive forever.\n2. Until a release, it is not the case that both are active; release optional.\n3. Before any release both inactive; release may never occur. Good.\n\nR054 \"Whenever the gate is open, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At every step where the gate is open, neither indicator is active.\n2. At every step where gate open, it is not the case that both are active.\n3. While gate open, both indicators inactive. Good.\n\nR055 \"Whenever a pulse occurs, the first and second indicators must not be active at the immediately following step.\" Interpretations:\n1. At the step immediately after any pulse, neither indicator is active.\n2. At the step immediately after any pulse, it is not the case that both are active.\n3. At the next step after a pulse, both indicators inactive. Good.\n\nR056 \"Whenever a pulse occurs, there must be a step at that time or later when the first and second indicators are not active.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, at some step at or after the pulse, neither indicator is active.\n2. For every pulse, at some step at or after the pulse, it is not the case that both are active.\n3. For every pulse, eventually after it both indicators are inactive. Good.\n\nR057 \"Whenever a pulse occurs, the first and second indicators must not be active until a release occurs at that step or later, and such a release is required.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every pulse, from the pulse step until a release at or after the pulse, neither indicator is active; a release eventually occurs.\n2. For every pulse, indicators not both active until a release at/after pulse; release required.\n3. For every pulse, both indicators inactive until the next release at/after pulse, and that release must occur. Good.\n\nR058 \"A check must occur at a step when the first and second indicators are not active.\" Interpretations:\n1. At some future step, a check occurs and neither indicator is active at that same step.\n2. At some future step, a check occurs and it is not the case that both indicators are active.\n3. There is a check step where both indicators are inactive. Good.\n\nR059 \"Requests are answered by a pulse or a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, a pulse or a flash occurs at some later step.\n2. For every request, a pulse or a flash occurs at the same step or later.\n3. For every request, either a pulse or a flash (or both) occurs at the next step. Good.\n\nR060 \"After a request, the next step must contain a pulse or a flash.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, at the immediately following step a pulse or a flash occurs.\n2. For every request, at the next step either a pulse or a flash (or both) occurs.\n3. For every request, the next step has at least one of pulse/flash. Good.\n\nR061 \"A request must be answered at once by a pulse or a flash, but not both.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, at the next step exactly one of pulse or flash occurs.\n2. For every request, at the same step exactly one of pulse or flash occurs.\n3. For every request, immediately after it either a pulse or a flash occurs, not both. Good.\n\nR062 \"Every request starts a pulse-then-flash or flash-then-pulse response, with the two events on consecutive steps.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, either a pulse occurs then a flash at the next step, or a flash occurs then a pulse at the next step.\n2. For every request, the response begins at the next step with one event and the other at the following step.\n3. For every request, there is a consecutive pair pulse-flash or flash-pulse after it. Good.\n\nR063 \"A request requires the first or second signal to stay active until a release, which must eventually occur.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, at least one of first/second signal is active from the request step until a release; release eventually occurs.\n2. For every request, either first or second signal remains active until the next release; release required.\n3. For every request, one of the signals stays active until some release, and a release must occur. Good.\n\nR064 \"A request requires the first or second signal to remain active unless released; release is optional.\" Interpretations:\n1. For every request, at least one signal stays active until a release if one occurs; if no release, it stays active forever.\n2. For every request, one of the signals remains